-- physicalities_ducet_rank_btree — makes collation-range queries an index scan.
--
-- ops.ducet_rank(coord) = (x^2 + y^2) * atom_window is IMMUTABLE and pure
-- arithmetic on the stored PointZM, so it is indexable. Without this index a
-- collation-range read is a full scan of every physicality followed by a sort:
-- MEASURED 2026-08-12, ops.ducet_ordered(12140, 6) took 1,444 ms to return six
-- rows, essentially all of it scan and sort, because LIMIT cannot help until
-- after the ordering is established.
--
-- The expression is written out rather than calling ops.ducet_rank, because a
-- functional index must reference a function that cannot be redefined under it;
-- inlining keeps the index independent of the ops schema's migration state.
-- ops.ducet_rank is the same expression and the planner matches it.
--
-- What this unlocks, and it is the tier-0 half of "precedes / follows / occurs
-- near": DUCET rank is the Hopf latitude parameter, so a btree range on this
-- expression IS a collation range. "Every atom that collates between X and Y"
-- becomes an index scan with no allkeys.txt lookup at runtime.
--
-- NOTE ON ORDER: this is a tier-0 organ. Composed entities are centroids, sit
-- inside the ball (radius_origin < 1, avg 0.373 at tier 2), and carry no
-- collation rank -- their value on this expression is meaningless, not merely
-- imprecise. Filter to tier 0 when reading, or the range returns interior noise.
--
-- APPLY OFF-INGEST. CREATE INDEX takes a ShareLock that blocks writers; on a
-- live seed this stalls the ingest for the duration of the build. Use
-- CONCURRENTLY if it must go in against a hot table.
CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS physicalities_ducet_rank_btree
    ON physicalities (((public.ST_X(coord)^2 + public.ST_Y(coord)^2)));
